  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 1235 AM MDT Tue Sep 15 2026\n\nModels in good agreement through Friday, with differences\nshowing up for the weekend into early next week. Active weather\nis expected for mid to late week, with uncertainty heading into\nthe weekend and early next week.\n\nWednesday through Friday...an upper low will slowly shift east\nout of the Pacific Northwest, while upper high pressure sits\nacross the southeast states. This will put southern Colorado\nunder south to southwesterly flow, with multiple upper wave\nmoving north across the state. PWAT values increase\nsignificantly to in excess of 1 inch, while dewpoints remain\nelevated in the 50s and lower 60s across the region. All point\nto efficient rain producing thunderstorms across the higher\nterrain. The main vort max train runs from Arizona, northeast\ninto northeast Colorado, with areas over the higher terrain,\nPalmer Divide and Raton Mesa favored. Issued a Flash Flood Watch\nfor the eastern San Juan Mountains and upper Rio Grande Valley\nfor Wednesday afternoon through Thursday evening. The first\nupper wave will lift north Wednesday afternoon and evening, with\nperiods of heavy rain expected across the Colorado Mountains. A\nbrief lull is expected Thursday morning, before another upper\nwave lifts into the southwest portion of the state Thursday\nafternoon. This will lead to another round of heavy rain\nproducing thunderstorms. Further east, showers and thunderstorms\nare expected, both Wednesday and Thursday afternoon and\nevenings, favoring the Palmer Divide and Raton Mesa. Models\ndiffer on how far east to track thunderstorms, but they could\nshift off the higher terrain and impact portions of the Plains\nthrough the overnight hours. Flash flooding may be possible on\narea burn scars and a watch may be needed.  \n\nThe upper low tracks eastward into the Northern Rockies on\nFriday shifting the main vort max feed east into eastern\nColorado. This will likely be the best chances for areas on the\nPlains to see shower and thunderstorm activity. Mountain areas\nwill continue to see periods of heavy rainfall on Friday, with a\ncontinued flash flood risk.  \n\nTemperatures look to remain cooler, with 70s and 80s each\nafternoon Wednesday through Friday. Overnight lows will fall\ninto the 50s and 60s across the Plains.\n\nSaturday through Tuesday...there is a high degree of spread\namong the deterministic guidance and ensembles heading into the\nweekend and early next week. The GFS remains activity with a\nquick moving upper ridge over the weekend, followed by another\nupper wave tracking across Colorado early next week. The GFS\nwould dry southern Colorado out over the weekend, with a return\nto wet conditions early next week. The ECMWF and Canadian are\nsimilar to the GFS with an upper ridge for the weekend, but\ndiffer by early next week, with a broad trough out west and\nsouthwesterly flow across Colorado. Overall, drier and warmer\nweather is forecast for the weekend, with active weather heading\ninto early next week with the GFS the wettest guidance compared\nto the ECMWF and Canadian.",
